---
import Base from "~/layouts/Base.astro";
import Support from "~/components/Support.astro";
import { candidates, categories, formatDate } from "~/lib/data";

const withStatement = candidates.filter((c) => c.sources.length > 0);
const withPositions = candidates.filter((c) => c.positions.length > 0);
const quoted = candidates.reduce((n, c) => n + c.positions.length, 0);
const statementOnly = withStatement.filter((c) => c.positions.length === 0);
const retrieved = withStatement[0]?.sources[0]?.retrieved;
---

<Base
  title="How this was built"
  description="Sources, tagging method, limitations and corrections policy for North Van Votes."
  ownSupport
>
  <h1>How I built this</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Don't take my word for any of it. Everything below is here so you can go check: where the quotes came from, how I sorted them, what I know this
    misses, and how to tell me I got something wrong.
  </p>
  <p style="color:var(--text-muted)">
    I'm Bahareh Ghadimi. I live in North Vancouver, I'm not running for
    anything, and nobody asked me to make this.
  </p>

  <div class="stat-row">
    <div class="stat"><b>{candidates.length}</b><span>candidates</span></div>
    <div class="stat"><b>{withStatement.length}</b><span>published a statement</span></div>
    <div class="stat"><b>{withPositions.length}</b><span>have a quoted position</span></div>
    <div class="stat"><b>{quoted}</b><span>quoted passages</span></div>
  </div>

  <h2>Where the words come from</h2>
  <p>
    Every quote is lifted straight from a candidate's own statement. That's the one they filed with their municipality by the nomination deadline, published
    exactly as they wrote it:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <a href="https://www.cnv.org/City-Hall/General-Local-Election/2026-General-Local-Election/Candidate-Profiles" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
        City of North Vancouver — Candidate Profiles
      </a>
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href="https://www.dnv.org/government-administration/see-who-is-running" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
        District of North Vancouver — See who is running
      </a>
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    I pulled them on {retrieved && formatDate(retrieved)}. Worth knowing: neither
    municipality fact-checks what candidates write, and neither do I. If a
    candidate claims something in their statement, that's their claim. Photos,
    contact details, nomination papers and campaign links come from the same two
    pages.
  </p>

  <h2>Why you can trust the quotes are real</h2>
  <p>
    This is the part I care most about getting right, so here's the whole
    mechanism.
  </p>
  <p>
    I use AI to sort quotes into topics, but it never writes a single word you
    read. I split each statement into numbered sentences, show the model those
    numbers, and it hands back a list of numbers. My code then cuts those exact
    characters out of the saved document.
  </p>
  <p>
    So a made-up quote isn't something I'm hoping to catch. It's something the
    setup can't produce, because there's no point where the AI is allowed to type. On
    top of that, every passage gets checked against its source before it goes
    live, and anything that doesn't match character for character gets thrown
    out. The worst mistake this can make is filing a real sentence under a topic
    you'd have filed differently.
  </p>

  <h2>Background as well as positions</h2>
  <p>
    Candidates write about two different things: what they'd do, and who they
    are. I pull both out, the same way and with the same guarantee, but I keep
    them apart on the page. A promise about transit and a job you held for
    twenty years are not the same kind of claim.
  </p>
  <p>
    Background covers public office and committees, career, education,
    community work, and how long someone has lived here. It's why a few
    candidates whose statement is a biography rather than a platform still have
    something on their page. I don't fact-check any of it, and neither does the
    municipality that published it. If someone says they chaired a committee,
    that's their word.
  </p>

  <h2>What I don't do</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Rate anyone.</strong> No scores, no grades, no endorsements.
      Everyone gets the same space and the same treatment.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Read anything into silence.</strong> If a candidate hasn't
      addressed a topic, all that means is I didn't find it in what I checked.
      It's a fact about my reading, not a verdict on them.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Reward being early in the alphabet.</strong> Candidates are
      shuffled, and the order changes each time you visit. There's an A–Z button
      if you'd rather.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Track you.</strong> No analytics script, no cookies, no accounts,
      and no third party ever sees that you were here. If you star candidates,
      that list never leaves your browser — I can't see it.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Campaign websites</h2>
  <p>
    As well as the filed statements I read the candidates' own campaign sites,
    where they listed one. 39 did. I crawled the pages most likely to hold a
    platform or a biography, at one request per second, and kept what I found.
    Quotes from those pages are marked <span class="srctag">Their campaign
    site</span> so you can tell them from a <span class="srctag srctag-official"
    >Filed statement</span>. Both are the candidate's own words; one went
    through a nomination filing and the other didn't.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>One of those sites I still can't read.</strong> The address Lisa
    Muri filed redirects to a Facebook page behind a login, which I won't
    scrape. Her page says so, because a thin page should not be mistaken for a
    quiet candidate. Several others were built with JavaScript and needed a real
    browser to read; that's now part of the pipeline.
  </p>
  <p>
    Kulvir Mann's site was in maintenance mode every time I checked, so his page
    here was nearly empty. He wrote on October 4 to say it was live again. It
    is, and his platform is now on his page. His menu is built in script with no
    links in the markup, so there was nothing for the crawler to follow; it now
    falls back to a site's own sitemap when that happens. His site also still
    carries his 2022 school trustee campaign in a separate section, which I
    don't quote from, because he is running for council this time and material
    from a previous race would read as though it were this one.
  </p>
  <p>
    Candidates repeat themselves across their own pages, which is normal and
    sensible for a campaign but made some pages here unreadable: one candidate
    came back with 124 passages, a sixth of them word-for-word identical. I
    now drop repeats and keep at most two passages per topic, preferring the
    filed statement and the more substantive quote.
  </p>

  <p>
    <strong>Two bugs of mine, both found because a candidate wrote in.</strong>
    My crawler compared web addresses literally, so a site that linked to itself
    without the "www." it was registered under looked like somewhere else
    entirely and every internal link got thrown away. I read the front page and
    stopped. Separately I threw out any quote under fifteen words as a fragment,
    which quietly deleted platforms written as short bullets, and a complete
    promise can take eleven words. Together these left Linda Munro with one
    position on a site full of policy. She emailed to ask why, and she was
    right. Fixing both added 117 passages across 36 candidates, so she was a
    long way from the only one it was happening to.
  </p>
  <p>
    I also used to hide a passage when a longer one elsewhere on the page
    repeated most of its words. That was meant to stop the same paragraph
    appearing twice, but it reached across topics, so a quote kept under one
    heading could delete a different heading from the same candidate. Passages
    are now compared only against others under the same topic, and where the
    candidate's own statement runs every priority into one block, I prefer their
    specific line over the block.
  </p>

  <p>
    The municipalities keep editing these pages after nominations close. A
    reader wrote on October 2 to say Stephan Nieweler's profile had appeared on
    the District's site, and re-checking found a filed statement and an official
    photo for him and for Catherine Pope that were not there when I first
    collected them. So I re-fetch both municipal pages rather than trusting what
    I gathered once, and the date above says when.
  </p>

  <h2>Websites the municipalities didn't list</h2>
  <p>
    The District publishes a website link for only some of its candidates. That
    meant people with a real campaign site — including the sitting mayor and
    three sitting councillors — showed up here with none, and looked quieter
    than they are. A reader spotted the same problem on the City side.
  </p>
  <p>
    So where a candidate has a site the official page doesn't list, I add it.
    Those came from <a href="https://localelections.ca/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
    localelections.ca</a> and from readers, each checked to make sure it loads
    and belongs to that candidate, and they're recorded in the repository with
    where they came from. It took the number of candidates with nothing at all
    from twelve down to six.
  </p>
  <p>
    The same goes for the other links. The District lists some of a candidate's
    channels and not others, which in one case meant the official page pointed
    at a personal profile while the page they actually campaign from was
    missing. Send me one and I'll add it, with two limits: it has to be a
    channel for this election, not a previous one, and I strip the tracking
    identifier off a share link before publishing it.
  </p>
  <p>
    Two candidates have a site listed that I link but deliberately don't read:
    Mike Little's address redirects to a Facebook page, and so does Lisa Muri's.
    Scraping Facebook behind a login isn't something I'll do.
  </p>

  <h2>Photos</h2>
  <p>
    The District publishes a photo for only some of its candidates, so people
    showed up here as a grey placeholder next to opponents with a headshot,
    which reads as someone who couldn't be bothered rather than a municipality
    that didn't publish one. Ten candidates are still in that position, eight of
    them in the District.
  </p>
  <p>
    So if you're a candidate and your page has no photo, send me one and I'll
    use it. That's open to all of you, not just whoever thinks to ask first. I
    crop what you send to the same square as the official ones and change
    nothing else, and each one is recorded in the repository with where it came
    from.
  </p>

  <h2>Where this falls short</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      These statements are short, a few hundred words each. Someone with a
      detailed platform on their own site will look thinner here than they
      really are. I always link their campaign site, so go read it.
    </li>
    <li>
      {statementOnly.length} candidates filed a statement that doesn't take a
      position on anything I track. Usually it's a biography rather than a
      platform. I say that plainly on their page instead of leaving them blank.
    </li>
    <li>
      The {categories.length} topics came from reading what candidates wrote
      about most often. There's nothing official about that list and it's
      definitely not complete.
    </li>
    <li>
      A sentence can genuinely belong to two topics, so you'll see the same
      quote turn up twice.
    </li>
    <li>
      I haven't touched news coverage, all-candidates meetings, social media or
      interviews. Candidates say plenty in those places. It's just not what this
      covers.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="elsewhere">Where else to look</h2>
  <p>
    This site reads two things: what candidates filed with their municipality,
    and what they wrote on their own campaign sites. Other people have gone and
    asked them questions directly, which I don't do. That work is worth your
    time, and it isn't mine:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>North Shore Bear Advocates</strong> asked every mayor and council
      candidate what they would actually do to help people and bears co-exist,
      and published the replies in full:
      <a href="https://docs.google.com/document/d/1L3Yk6973Qx1mddPKAix5mp1UhlDBRGCfn6_KR03FQ18/edit" target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow">District of North Vancouver</a>
      and
      <a href="https://docs.google.com/document/d/1YV9vVgJsZ_X8P2o2Mi_UOafmWpk6QWRWMeh4faAsNHQ/edit" target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow">City of North Vancouver</a>.
      Worth knowing: several people who have very little on this site wrote
      back to them at length, including candidates whose pages here are empty
      because they filed no statement and listed no website.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href="https://en.votemate.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow">VoteMate</a>
      lets candidates upload their own profile. Take-up here is patchy, but
      where someone has filled it in there is more than I have.
    </li>
    <li>
      <a href="https://localelections.ca/election_candidates/86_2026_candidates.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener nofollow">localelections.ca</a>
      is a plain directory of who is running across BC, and it is where several
      of the campaign websites on this site came from.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Listing these is not an endorsement of the people who made them or of how
    they framed their questions. One difference is worth saying out loud: a
    questionnaire records who replied and who didn't, and this site deliberately
    doesn't. An empty topic here only ever means I didn't find something in what
    I read. It never means someone was asked and declined.
  </p>

  <h2 id="corrections">Corrections</h2>
  <p>
    If a quote misrepresents you, a topic's wrong, a link's broken, or something
    on the voting page has gone stale, tell me. I'll fix it within 48 hours, or
    pull the item down while I check. Candidates, campaigns and readers all get
    the same treatment here.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href="mailto:corrections@northvanvotes.ca">corrections@northvanvotes.ca</a>
    — or <a href="mailto:hello@northvanvotes.ca">hello@northvanvotes.ca</a> for
    anything else. Both reach the same person.
  </p>
  <p class="candidate-meta">
    Anything factual, like a broken link or a date I typed wrong, I'll just fix. But
    if you ask me to soften or remove a quote that accurately reproduces your
    own published statement, I'll say no and tell you why. You filed it publicly,
    and the link next to it goes straight to the original.
  </p>

  <h2>What my server records</h2>
  <p>
    One thing I should be straight about, since the point above could be read
    too generously. My web server keeps an access log, the way essentially every
    web server does: which page was requested, when, and the site you followed a
    link from. That's how I can tell whether anyone is actually using this.
  </p>
  <p>
    There is no script on the page doing it, nothing is stored in your browser,
    and nothing is sent to any other company. When I read those logs, IP
    addresses are cut short first, so I can see roughly how many different
    people visited without being able to pick one out. I'm not building profiles
    and there is nothing to sell.
  </p>

  <h2>Independence</h2>
  <p>
    I'm not affiliated with any candidate, campaign, elector organization,
    political party, municipality or school district. Nobody pays me to run
    this and nobody gets a say in what's on it.
  </p>

  <Support variant="block" />

  <div class="notice">
    <p>
      <strong>Regulatory status.</strong> BC's Local Elections Campaign Financing
      Act governs third-party election advertising, and during the campaign
      period that definition reaches communications taking a position on an issue
      a candidate is associated with.
    </p>
    <p>
      Elections BC's own
      {" "}<a href="https://elections.bc.ca/docs/lecfa/4500003-Local-election-advertising-quick-reference-sheet.pdf" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
      advertising quick reference sheet</a> lists "websites or blogs" and "any
      free communication on the internet" under <em>not</em> election
      advertising. The line it draws is placement cost: a newspaper ad, a
      billboard, a paid social post, a robocall. This site has no placement cost,
      carries no advertising, is never paid to be promoted, and neither promotes
      nor opposes any candidate.
    </p>
    <p style="margin-bottom:0">
      Elections BC can be reached at 1-800-661-8683 or
      {" "}<a href="mailto:electoral.finance@elections.bc.ca">electoral.finance@elections.bc.ca</a>.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>The code is open</h2>
  <p>
    It's a small pipeline: download the public documents, save them, pull the
    quotes out, build the pages. I keep every document I downloaded next to the
    code, so anyone can rebuild this from scratch and check my work against the
    same files I used.
  </p>
</Base>
